From: Prabhsimran Singh <prabhsimrans@nvidia.com>
Subject: llama: first-fit KV cells for batched serving

The slot search started at the cache head left by earlier allocations, so the
same request landed on different cells depending on what ran before it, and
attention results depend on where the cells are (the covered KV span and its
padding change the kernel launch). With batched serving (n_kv bucket set) the
search starts at cell 0: identical requests on the same occupancy get the same
cells.

Upstream: candidate.

diff --git a/src/llama-kv-cache.cpp b/src/llama-kv-cache.cpp
index 2c022b106d1e39d60d2663ea08e143b500e26476..cc7a8410d813f15e5de480c9919bc47eb5c12b0a 100644
--- a/src/llama-kv-cache.cpp
+++ b/src/llama-kv-cache.cpp
@@ -1005,7 +1005,9 @@ llama_kv_cache::slot_info llama_kv_cache::find_slot(const llama_ubatch & ubatch,
 
         // if we have enough unused cells before the current head ->
         //   better to start searching from the beginning of the cache, hoping to fill it
-        if (head_cur > cells.get_used() + 2*n_tokens) {
+        // Batched serving always searches from the beginning (first fit): attention results depend on where a
+        // sequence's cells are, so placement must follow the cache's occupancy, not its allocation history.
+        if (n_kv_bucket > 0 || head_cur > cells.get_used() + 2*n_tokens) {
             head_cur = 0;
         }
 
